                %li
                  %a{:href => "#HigherGaugeTheoryOfTheGreenSchwarzMechanismReferences"} Higher gauge theory of the Green-Schwarz mechanism
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            (such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            or the fields that induce the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nuclear+force"} nuclear force
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field (in the sense of physics)
            which is locally represented by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-form"} differential 1-form
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potential"}> gauge potential
            ”) and whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            is locally a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Faraday+tensor"} Faraday tensor
          %p
            Roughly speaking, a
            %em higher gauge field
            is similarly a field which is locally represented by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            of higher degree.
          %p
            An explanation as to why an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potential"} gauge potential
            given locally by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-form"} differential 1-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+particle"} charged particle
            is a 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curve"} curve
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \colon \Sigma_1 \to X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            of such curves is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integration of 1-forms
            over curves:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mi ℏ
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi gauge
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi γ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi P
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mi γ
                      %mo *
                    %mi A
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} S_{gauge})
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\gamma
                  \\mapsto
                  P \exp\left(
                  \\int_{\Sigma_1} \gamma^\ast A
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            map or the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            map, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_1
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
            of the particle obtained from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variation
            of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+force"} Lorentz force
            which is exerted by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            on the particle.
          %p
            When one generalizes in this picture from 0-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            with 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
            to
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-branes"} p-branes
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolumes"} worldvolumes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_{p+1} \colon \Sigma_{p+1} \to X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+n-form"} differential (p+1)-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{p+1}
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mi ℏ
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi higher
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi gauge
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi γ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi P
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mrow
                            %mi p
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                    %msubsup
                      %mi γ
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} S_{higher\,gauge})
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\gamma
                  \\mapsto
                  P \exp\left(
                  \\int_{\Sigma_{p+1}} \gamma_{p+1}^\ast A_{p+1}
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux"} flux
            of such a higher gauge field is, accordingly, locally the
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+2)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{p+2}
          %p
            The archetypical example of such a higher gauge field is the (hypothetical)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            (a precursor of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axion"} axion
            field under
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for the string is accordingly, locally, of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mi ℏ
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi stringy
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi gauge
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi γ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi P
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mn 2
                    %msubsup
                      %mi γ
                      %mn 2
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} S_{stringy\,gauge})
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\gamma
                  \\mapsto
                  P \exp\left(
                  \\int_{\Sigma_{2}} \gamma_2^\ast B_2
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This continues: next one may consider “2-branes”, i.e.
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membranes"} membranes
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
          %p But there is an important further aspect to higher gauge fields which makes this simple picture of higher degree differential forms drastically more rich:
          %p
            Where an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1 \mapsto A'_1
            given locally by smooth functions (0-forms)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda_0
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{dR}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A'_1 = A_1 + d_{dR} \lambda_0
          %p
            so a higher gauge field has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+transformations"} higher gauge transformations
            given locally by
            = succeed "-forms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi λ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda_p
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mi p
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A'_{p+1} = A_{p+1} + d_{dR} \lambda_{p}
                  \\,.
          %p
            But for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \gt 0
            then a crucial new effect appears: these gauge transformations, being higher differential forms themselves, have “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+transformation"}> gauge-of-gauge transformations
            ” between them, given by lower degree forms.
          %p
            This phenomenon implies that higher gauge fields have a rich
            %em global
            (“topological”) structure, witnessed by the higher analog of their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sectors"} instanton sectors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-brane"} p-brane
            may couple is
            %em locally
            given by a
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{p+1}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+charts"} coordinate charts
            this form gauge transforms by a
            = succeed "-form," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-form," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p-1)
          %p
            The global structure for higher gauge fields obtained by carrying out this globalization via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+transformations"} higher gauge transformations
            is the higher analog of that of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
            or, more generally, a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+infinity-bundle"} principal infinity-bundle
          %p
            In fact the situation that there is just one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potential"} gauge potential
            of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+1)
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+2)
            is just the simplest case, the “ordinary” case. More abstractly one says that such higher gauge fields are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
          %p
            More generally it may happen in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            that the gauge potential is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+linear+combination"} formal linear combination
            of differential forms in various degrees.
          %p
            The canonical example of this phenomenon is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+transformations"} higher gauge transformations
            in this situation to find the correct global structure (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sector"}> instanton sector
            ”) of the higher gauge field, then one finds that this now is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
            condition for the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Freed00"} Freed 00
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomaly+cancellation"} anomaly cancellation
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            depends on being careful about the global nature of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theoretic
            higher gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            (
            %a{:href => "#DistlerFreedMoore09"}> Distler-Freed-Moore 09
            )
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
            Among ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            there is
          %ol
            %li
              %p “metric” gauge theory:
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell+theory"}> Maxwell theory
                    ,
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological
                gauge theory:
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            (as well as mixed cases, such as the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ABJM+model"} ABJM model
          %p
            While the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            of these theories are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            of the same kind in all cases (modeled by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            on
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"}> pseudo
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"}> gravity
            ) while the second does not (is a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological field theory
          %p
            Moreover, even though
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell+theory"} Maxwell theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"}> electromagnetism
            ) may be understood as the special case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            specialized to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
          %p Some example of higher gauge fields…
          %ol
            %li
              %p …of higher Maxwell type:
              %p
                the fields appearing in higher-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                theories:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
                    (
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"}> B-field
                    )
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            %li
              %p …of higher Chern-Simons type:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AKSZ+sigma-models"} AKSZ sigma-models
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field
          %h3#HigherGaugeTheoryOfMaxwellType Higher gauge theories of Maxwell type
          %p Consider
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi d
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 1 + d \in \mathbb{N}_{\geq 2}
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                dimension,
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^D
                a
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} manifold
            %li
              %p
                with
                = succeed "-operator" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star"} Hodge star
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi dR
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi dR
                        %mrow
                          %mi D
                          %mo −
                          %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\star \,\colon\,
                      \\Omega^p_{dR}(X^D) \to \Omega^{D-p}_{dR}(X^D)
              %p
                satisfying (see
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "Hodge+star+operator#eq:HodgeSquareOnRiemannian"} there
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi D
                          %mo −
                          %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\star \, \star = - (-1)^{p(D-p)}
